{:edb/db-before-t 676 :edb/db-after-t 677 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533989 50 #inst "2026-10-06T00:00:40.549Z" 13194139533989 true] [17592186083191 1020 #edb/ref 17592186275770 13194139533989 true] [17592186083191 1020 #edb/ref 17592186275774 13194139533989 true] [17592186083191 1020 #edb/ref 17592186275784 13194139533989 true] [17592186083192 1020 #edb/ref 17592186275763 13194139533989 true] [17592186083192 1020 #edb/ref 17592186275777 13194139533989 true] [17592186275763 1017 #edb/ref 17592186275764 13194139533989 true] [17592186275763 1089 "q-124570" 13194139533989 true] [17592186275763 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533989 true] [17592186275763 1100 "Consider the positive infinite geometric sequence that has a first term equal to $9$ and a third term equal to $4.$ What is the sum to infinity of the terms of this sequence?" 13194139533989 true] [17592186275763 1113 #uuid "d05dedb0-ea9f-500b-8b24-c5b96491cf54" 13194139533989 true] [17592186275763 1145 "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFrom the question statement we have ${a}_{1}=9$ and ${a}_{3}=4.$ We can find the common ratio using the following formula:\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the known information into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{3-1} & =\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ {r}^{2} & ={(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we have taken the positive square root since we're told that the sequence is positive.\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{(1-\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{(\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =9\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533989 true] [17592186275764 1000 #edb/ref 17592186275765 13194139533989 tru